From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: media: cedrus: drive the VE decode-time scale-down

The H713 display pipeline has no downscaler. An exhaustive census of the MIPS
display firmware found exactly three ratio-carrying blocks -- the proc scaler at
0x05180000 (upscale only, measured), the panel down-scaler at 0x051c0138
(vertical only) and a capture-side scaler at 0x06940000 (input path, cannot be
fed from DRAM). None can do 1920x1080 -> 1280x720.

Static RE of the vendor Android stack says why: the vendor never downscales on
the display side. It downscales AT THE DECODER. libOmxVdec.so exports
anSetScaleDownParam, libvdecoder.so exports ConfigExtraScaleInfo and logs
"scale wxh = %dx%d", and libawh264.so carries H264ConfigureScaleRotateRegister,
which writes reg40/reg44/reg48.

Those numbers are offsets within the H.264 ENGINE block, not the top-level VE
register file: mainline already names the first of them VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL
(0x240 = VE_ENGINE_DEC_H264 + 0x40) and leaves 0x244/0x248 as an unnamed gap,
and the H.265 engine carries the same trio as VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_{CTRL,LUMA_ADDR,
CHROMA_ADDR}. This patch names the two H.264 address registers and drives all
three.

Confirmed on the board. The unit is real, and the control word decodes as two
independent 2-bit fields:

    VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL[9:8]    horizontal   0 = 1:1, 1 = 1/2, 2 = 1/4
    VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL[11:10]  vertical     0 = 1:1, 1 = 1/2, 2 = 1/4
    3 in either field produces a partial, malformed write.

Twelve control words were swept against a 1920x1088 source and every output
size matched that model exactly, including two nulls and two invalid-field
partials; 960x544, 1920x544 and 960x1088 were rendered and visually confirmed
correctly proportioned. So the scale-down is power-of-two per axis -- which
matches H264ComputeScaleRatio returning 0/1/2 for none/half/quarter -- and it
cannot produce 1280x720. The arbitrary-ratio path (H264ConfigNewScaler, a
256-byte coefficient table plus VE+0x20/0xcc/0xe4..0xfc) is untouched here.

Three things had to be right before any of this produced a picture, and each
one failed silently first:

  - The registers must be programmed AFTER the codec's own setup. That is where
    cedrus_engine_enable() writes VE_MODE; programming a disabled engine leaves
    the address registers reading back zero. Hence sd_stage.
  - The addresses go in raw, unshifted, unlike several other engine registers.
    Hence sd_shift, and the readback that settles it.
  - The secondary output format defaults to TILED_32_NV12. Selecting linear
    NV12 needs the EXT table (VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN[31:30] = 1) and then
    VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT[3:0] = 4. Clearing those two bits selects the tiled
    default, which yields a striped dump from a scaler that is working
    correctly. Hence sd_fmt.

This is a harness, not an implementation: every unknown is a module parameter
so the encoding can be swept from userspace without a rebuild, and the output
buffer is exposed at /sys/kernel/debug/cedrus_sd_buf. It is re-poisoned with
0xa5 whenever sd_ctrl changes, so each candidate is judged against a clean
buffer and "wrote nothing" stays distinguishable from "wrote black". A shadow
copy is taken when the decoding context is released, so the result outlives the
player that produced it.

    sunxi_cedrus.sd_w=1920 sd_h=2176    target size; 0 (default) disables
                                        everything and the driver is unchanged
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_ctrl=0x500          raw value for VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_fmt=1               select linear NV12 rather than tiled
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_stage=1             program after the codec setup
    sunxi_cedrus.sd_shift=0             address shift

Default off: with sd_w=0 not a single register write changes.

Driven by tools/video/ve-scaledown-sweep.sh.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---

diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.c
@@ -410,6 +410,9 @@
 	v4l2_ctrl_handler_free(&ctx->hdl);
 	kfree(ctx->ctrls);
 
+	/* EXPERIMENT: release the scale-down secondary output buffer. */
+	cedrus_sd_free(ctx);
+
 	v4l2_fh_exit(&ctx->fh);
 
 	kfree(ctx);
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
@@ -93,11 +93,20 @@
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_copy_metadata(run.src, run.dst, true);
 
 	cedrus_dst_format_set(dev, &ctx->dst_fmt);
+	/* EXPERIMENT: point the scale-down unit at our own buffer. */
+	cedrus_sd_setup(ctx, 0);
 
 	error = ctx->current_codec->setup(ctx, &run);
 	if (error)
 		v4l2_err(&ctx->dev->v4l2_dev,
 			 "Failed to setup decoding job: %d\n", error);
+	else
+		/*
+		 * The codec's setup runs cedrus_engine_enable(), so this is the
+		 * first point at which the engine is actually on -- which is
+		 * where the vendor programs scale/rotate.
+		 */
+		cedrus_sd_setup(ctx, 1);
 
 	/* Complete request(s) controls if needed. */
 
@@ -110,6 +119,9 @@
 		schedule_delayed_work(&dev->watchdog_work,
 				      msecs_to_jiffies(2000));
 
+		/* EXPERIMENT: did the scale-down values survive to the trigger? */
+		cedrus_sd_verify(ctx);
+
 		ctx->current_codec->trigger(ctx);
 	} else {
 		v4l2_m2m_buf_done_and_job_finish(ctx->dev->m2m_dev,
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -121,6 +121,14 @@
 	struct cedrus_dec_ops		*current_codec;
 	unsigned int			bit_depth;
 
+	/*
+	 * EXPERIMENT: secondary (scale-down) output buffer. Allocated lazily
+	 * when sd_w/sd_h are set, freed with the context.
+	 */
+	void				*sd_buf;
+	dma_addr_t			sd_buf_dma;
+	size_t				sd_buf_size;
+
 	struct v4l2_ctrl_handler	hdl;
 	struct v4l2_ctrl		**ctrls;
 
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -16,6 +16,8 @@
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_reserved_mem.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/clk.h>
@@ -31,6 +33,261 @@
 #include "cedrus_hw.h"
 #include "cedrus_regs.h"
 
+/*
+ * EXPERIMENT: the VE scale-down secondary output.
+ *
+ * Inert unless sd_w and sd_h are both non-zero: with the defaults not one
+ * register write changes, so a kernel carrying this behaves exactly as before.
+ */
+static unsigned int sd_w;
+static unsigned int sd_h;
+static unsigned int sd_ctrl;
+static unsigned int sd_fmt;
+static unsigned int sd_stage = 1;
+static unsigned int sd_shift;
+module_param(sd_w, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_w, "EXPERIMENT: scale-down target width (0 = disabled)");
+module_param(sd_h, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_h, "EXPERIMENT: scale-down target height (0 = disabled)");
+module_param(sd_ctrl, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_ctrl,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: raw value for VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL (encoding unknown)");
+module_param(sd_fmt, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_fmt, "EXPERIMENT: also set the secondary output format bits");
+module_param(sd_stage, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_stage,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: program at 0 = before the codec setup, 1 = after it (default; engine enabled)");
+module_param(sd_shift, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(sd_shift,
+		 "EXPERIMENT: right-shift applied to the output addresses (0 = raw, 8 = >>8)");
+
+static struct dentry *cedrus_sd_debugfs;
+static struct cedrus_ctx *cedrus_sd_ctx;
+static unsigned int sd_ctrl_last;
+static bool sd_ctrl_armed;
+static bool sd_log_once;
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+/*
+ * A shadow copy of the last secondary output, taken when the decoding context
+ * goes away. Without it the result is only readable while a decode is in
+ * flight, because the DMA buffer belongs to the context: closing the decoder
+ * would destroy the very thing the experiment is trying to look at.
+ */
+static void *cedrus_sd_shadow;
+static size_t cedrus_sd_shadow_size;
+
+static ssize_t cedrus_sd_read(struct file *file, char __user *buf,
+			      size_t count, loff_t *ppos)
+{
+	struct cedrus_ctx *ctx;
+	ssize_t ret = 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	ctx = cedrus_sd_ctx;
+	if (ctx && ctx->sd_buf)
+		ret = simple_read_from_buffer(buf, count, ppos, ctx->sd_buf,
+					      ctx->sd_buf_size);
+	else if (cedrus_sd_shadow)
+		ret = simple_read_from_buffer(buf, count, ppos,
+					      cedrus_sd_shadow,
+					      cedrus_sd_shadow_size);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct file_operations cedrus_sd_fops = {
+	.owner	= THIS_MODULE,
+	.read	= cedrus_sd_read,
+	.llseek	= default_llseek,
+};
+
+/*
+ * The node is owned by the driver, not by a decoding context: a debugfs file
+ * whose fops live in this module must not outlive the module, and one created
+ * per context would vanish the moment a player exits.
+ */
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_init(void)
+{
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs = debugfs_create_file("cedrus_sd_buf", 0400, NULL,
+						NULL, &cedrus_sd_fops);
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_exit(void)
+{
+	debugfs_remove(cedrus_sd_debugfs);
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs = NULL;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+	vfree(cedrus_sd_shadow);
+	cedrus_sd_shadow = NULL;
+	cedrus_sd_shadow_size = 0;
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_free(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	if (!ctx->sd_buf)
+		return;
+
+	mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	if (cedrus_sd_ctx == ctx) {
+		if (cedrus_sd_shadow_size != ctx->sd_buf_size) {
+			vfree(cedrus_sd_shadow);
+			cedrus_sd_shadow = vmalloc(ctx->sd_buf_size);
+			cedrus_sd_shadow_size = cedrus_sd_shadow ?
+						ctx->sd_buf_size : 0;
+		}
+		if (cedrus_sd_shadow)
+			memcpy(cedrus_sd_shadow, ctx->sd_buf,
+			       cedrus_sd_shadow_size);
+
+		cedrus_sd_ctx = NULL;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+
+	dma_free_coherent(ctx->dev->dev, ctx->sd_buf_size, ctx->sd_buf,
+			  ctx->sd_buf_dma);
+	ctx->sd_buf = NULL;
+	ctx->sd_buf_size = 0;
+}
+
+void cedrus_sd_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, unsigned int stage)
+{
+	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
+	unsigned int w = ALIGN(sd_w, 16);
+	unsigned int h = ALIGN(sd_h, 16);
+	size_t luma, size;
+	bool log = false;
+	u32 reg;
+
+	if (!sd_w || !sd_h)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Which side of the codec's own setup to program from. The vendor
+	 * configures scale/rotate as part of the decode configuration, i.e.
+	 * after cedrus_engine_enable() has written VE_MODE; programming a
+	 * disabled engine would be a null result that means nothing. Kept as a
+	 * parameter so both orderings can be compared without a rebuild.
+	 */
+	if (stage != sd_stage)
+		return;
+
+	luma = (size_t)w * h;
+	size = luma * 3 / 2;
+
+	if (ctx->sd_buf && ctx->sd_buf_size != size)
+		cedrus_sd_free(ctx);
+
+	if (!ctx->sd_buf) {
+		ctx->sd_buf = dma_alloc_coherent(dev->dev, size,
+						 &ctx->sd_buf_dma, GFP_KERNEL);
+		if (!ctx->sd_buf) {
+			dev_warn(dev->dev, "sd: cannot allocate %zu bytes\n",
+				 size);
+			return;
+		}
+		ctx->sd_buf_size = size;
+		mutex_lock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+		cedrus_sd_ctx = ctx;
+		mutex_unlock(&cedrus_sd_lock);
+		sd_ctrl_armed = false;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Re-poison whenever the swept value changes, so each candidate is
+	 * judged against a clean buffer rather than against whatever the
+	 * previous candidate left behind. A scaler that never runs leaves the
+	 * pattern intact, which separates "wrote nothing" from "wrote black" --
+	 * a flat fill is ambiguous on this hardware. Logging here rather than
+	 * at allocation means one line per swept value, not one per decode.
+	 */
+	if (!sd_ctrl_armed || sd_ctrl != sd_ctrl_last) {
+		memset(ctx->sd_buf, 0xa5, ctx->sd_buf_size);
+		sd_ctrl_last = sd_ctrl;
+		sd_ctrl_armed = true;
+		log = true;
+		sd_log_once = true;
+		dev_info(dev->dev,
+			 "sd: %ux%u buffer %zu bytes at %pad, ctrl %#x, stage %u\n",
+			 w, h, size, &ctx->sd_buf_dma, sd_ctrl, sd_stage);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * H.264 frame addresses go into the engine raw (see cedrus_buf_addr),
+	 * but several other engine registers take them >> 8. Rather than guess,
+	 * write under sd_shift and read back: a register that stores 0xff000000
+	 * as 0x00ff0000 has told us its own convention.
+	 */
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR,
+		     (u32)(ctx->sd_buf_dma >> sd_shift));
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR,
+		     (u32)((ctx->sd_buf_dma + luma) >> sd_shift));
+
+	if (sd_fmt) {
+		/*
+		 * The secondary output format is a two-level selector: the top
+		 * two bits of VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN pick the table, and only the
+		 * EXT table carries linear NV12, in VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT[3:0].
+		 * Clearing those two bits selects TILED_32_NV12 (the reset
+		 * default), which is what produced a striped dump on the first
+		 * hit -- the scaler was running correctly and being read wrong.
+		 */
+		reg = cedrus_read(dev, VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN);
+		reg = (reg & ~(0x3u << 30)) | VE_SECONDARY_OUT_FMT_EXT;
+		cedrus_write(dev, VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN, reg);
+
+		reg = cedrus_read(dev, VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT);
+		reg = (reg & ~0xfu) | VE_SECONDARY_OUT_FMT_EXT_NV12;
+		cedrus_write(dev, VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT, reg);
+	}
+
+	cedrus_write(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL, sd_ctrl);
+
+	if (log) {
+		/*
+		 * Read the registers back. A null result from a write that never
+		 * landed is indistinguishable from a null result from a scaler
+		 * that does not exist -- and only one of those is a finding.
+		 */
+		dev_info(dev->dev,
+			 "sd: wrote  ctrl=%#010x luma=%#010x chroma=%#010x  VE_MODE=%#010x\n",
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR),
+			 cedrus_read(dev, VE_MODE));
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Read the scale-down registers back at the point the decode is actually
+ * triggered, which is what says whether the values survived the codec's own
+ * setup -- cedrus_engine_enable() rewrites VE_MODE, and the engine is disabled
+ * between frames.
+ */
+void cedrus_sd_verify(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
+
+	if (!sd_w || !sd_h || !ctx->sd_buf || !sd_log_once)
+		return;
+
+	sd_log_once = false;
+
+	dev_info(dev->dev,
+		 "sd: attrig ctrl=%#010x luma=%#010x chroma=%#010x  VE_MODE=%#010x\n",
+		 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL),
+		 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR),
+		 cedrus_read(dev, VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR),
+		 cedrus_read(dev, VE_MODE));
+}
+
 int cedrus_engine_enable(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
 {
 	u32 reg = 0;
@@ -397,6 +654,8 @@
 			goto err_pm;
 	}
 
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs_init();
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_pm:
@@ -411,6 +670,8 @@
 
 void cedrus_hw_remove(struct cedrus_dev *dev)
 {
+	cedrus_sd_debugfs_exit();
+
 	pm_runtime_disable(dev->dev);
 	if (!pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev->dev))
 		cedrus_hw_suspend(dev->dev);
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.h
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.h
@@ -25,6 +25,12 @@
 int cedrus_hw_suspend(struct device *device);
 int cedrus_hw_resume(struct device *device);
 
+void cedrus_sd_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, unsigned int stage);
+void cedrus_sd_verify(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx);
+void cedrus_sd_free(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx);
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_init(void);
+void cedrus_sd_debugfs_exit(void);
+
 int cedrus_hw_probe(struct cedrus_dev *dev);
 void cedrus_hw_remove(struct cedrus_dev *dev);
 
diff -u --recursive a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_regs.h
@@ -607,6 +607,17 @@
 #define VE_H264_VLD_LEN			0x238
 #define VE_H264_VLD_END			0x23c
 #define VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL		0x240
+/*
+ * The two registers following VE_H264_SDROT_CTRL are the scale-down output
+ * addresses. Mainline leaves the gap unnamed, but three sources agree on what
+ * lives there: the vendor libawh264.so exports sd_rotate_ctrl_reg40,
+ * sd_rotate_buf_addr_reg44 and sd_rotate_chroma_buf_addr_reg48, whose numbers
+ * are offsets within this engine block (0x200), matching SDROT_CTRL at 0x240;
+ * and the H.265 engine carries the same trio as VE_DEC_H265_SDRT_{CTRL,
+ * LUMA_ADDR,CHROMA_ADDR} at its own +0x50/0x54/0x58.
+ */
+#define VE_H264_SDROT_LUMA_ADDR		0x244
+#define VE_H264_SDROT_CHROMA_ADDR	0x248
 #define VE_H264_OUTPUT_FRAME_IDX	0x24c
 #define VE_H264_EXTRA_BUFFER1		0x250
 #define VE_H264_EXTRA_BUFFER2		0x254
